//? Title: Tied Autoencoders: One Matrix, Two Gradient Paths
//? Subtitle: Live transposed weights, matched initial functions and an independent-decoder control
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Weight tying constrains a decoder matrix to the live transpose of its encoder matrix.
This report derives the two contributions to the shared gradient, checks them on a small
hand calculation, then trains the original 784–32–784 ReLU/sigmoid MNIST model and a matched
untied control. Both start with identical codes and predictions and receive ninety-six Adam
updates. The tied model has 25,904 independent scalars against 50,992 for the control; held-out
MSE is 0.04965 and 0.04900 respectively, versus 0.06780 for the fitting mean. The comparison
illustrates a shared constraint under one short seed/budget, not equal capacity, a general
ranking, a classifier or a measured compression bitrate.
)
\toc

> A constraint on one parameter, not a copied initialization

For row inputs X with d features, encoder W has shape [d,k]. Define

$$
H=\operatorname{ReLU}(XW+b_e),\qquad D=W^\top,\qquad
R=\sigma(HD+b_d),\qquad L=\frac1{Bd}\|R-X\|_F^2.
$$
\alttext(The encoder uses W and ReLU, while the sigmoid decoder uses the same W transposed. The two biases are independent; loss averages squared errors over every row and pixel.)

The [Theano autoencoder tutorial](https://deeplearningtutorials.readthedocs.io/en/latest/dA.html)
implements a shared W and symbolic W transpose. This report retains the migrated experiment's
ReLU code, ordinary Glorot weights and mean squared error without corruption. A transpose is
not generally an inverse: neither W transpose W nor W W transpose must be identity, and ReLU
can discard information. The independent control initially copies the numerical transpose,
then trains its decoder as a separate leaf. Copying initial values alone does not enforce tying.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/tied_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(52, 1100);
  d.node("x", {0, 0}, "Image rows X\n784 pixels")
      .node("h", {6, 0}, "ReLU code H\n32 activities")
      .node("r", {12, 0}, "Sigmoid reconstruction R\n784 raw pixels")
      .node("w", {6, 3}, "ONE encoder/W parameter\n25,088 shared coordinates")
      .node("e", {0, -3}, "Encoder gradient contribution\nX transpose · encoder delta")
      .node("d", {12, -3}, "Decoder gradient contribution\ndecoder delta transpose · H")
      .node("g", {6, -6}, "Sum both contributions\nONE Adam state per W coordinate")
      .edge("x", "h")
      .edge("h", "r")
      .edge("w", "h", "W")
      .edge("w", "r", "live W transpose")
      .edge("h", "e")
      .edge("r", "d")
      .edge("e", "g")
      .edge("d", "g")
      .save_svg("build/tied_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/tied_method.svg)
// result_end

\caption(One matrix feeds both differentiable uses. Independent encoder and decoder biases remain trainable.)
\alttext(The single shared W connects to the encoder and decoder. Two derivative branches join before one optimizer update.)

The canonical `models::TiedAutoencoder` returns owning encoder_weight, decoder_weight, code
and reconstruction handles. With tied=true there is no decoder/W leaf. Every call takes a live
transpose without detaching it; autograd sums both paths. A store containing an independent
decoder/W is rejected in tied mode. Width/input/dtype checks precede creation; incompatible
existing shapes can fail later. Finite Float32/64 rows and strides are supported; stored or
computed nonfinite values are not sanitized. Fitting, selection and matching are caller policy.

> The shared gradient and a hand-checkable example

Let Z=XW+b_e and \(E=2(R-X)\odot R\odot(1-R)/(Bd)\). At positive ReLU
preactivations, or with the implementation's zero derivative at Z≤0, set
\(A=(EW)\odot\mathbf1\{Z>0\}\). Then

$$
\frac{\partial L}{\partial W}=\underbrace{X^\top A}_{\text{encoder use}}
+\underbrace{E^\top H}_{\text{decoder use}},\quad
\frac{\partial L}{\partial b_e}=\sum_b A_b,\quad
\frac{\partial L}{\partial b_d}=\sum_b E_b.
$$
\alttext(The shared matrix derivative sums the ordinary encoder derivative and the transpose of the decoder derivative. Bias gradients sum their corresponding deltas over rows.)

For d=3,k=2 use W rows [0.2,0.1], [−0.3,0.4], [0.5,−0.2], b_e=[0.1,0.2],
b_d=[−0.1,0.05,0.2], and X rows [0.1,0.2,0.7], [0.8,0.3,0.4]. The code rows
are [0.41,0.15] and [0.37,0.32]. Their reconstruction rows are approximately
[0.49925,0.49675,0.59267] and [0.50150,0.51674,0.57957].
The nonzero vector [−0.14,0.09,0.11] has zero product with W, so sufficiently small
perturbations along it preserve the code and reconstruction: the transpose cannot invert all
inputs. All code preactivations here are positive, so the derivative avoids the ReLU kink. The program
checks all four code values, six predictions and eleven derivatives against scalar arithmetic.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/tied_toy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n                    = dm::nn;
  const auto                type = n::DType::Float64;
  n::ParameterStore         p(1861, type);
  const std::vector<double> w{.2, .1, -.3, .4, .5, -.2}, eb{.1, .2}, db{-.1, .05, .2},
      v{.1, .2, .7, .8, .3, .4};
  const auto x = n::from_vector(v, {2, 3});
  n::models::TiedAutoencoder(p, x, 3, 2);
  p.set("encoder/W", n::from_vector(w, {3, 2}));
  p.set("encoder/b", n::from_vector(eb, {2}));
  p.set("decoder/b", n::from_vector(db, {3}));
  const auto net       = n::models::TiedAutoencoder(p, x, 3, 2);
  const auto shifted   = x + .5 * n::tile(n::tensor({-.14, .09, .11}, {1, 3}, type), {2, 1});
  const auto same_code = n::models::TiedAutoencoder(p, shifted, 3, 2);
  if (n::max(n::abs(net.code - same_code.code)).item() > 1e-14 ||
      n::max(n::abs(net.reconstruction - same_code.reconstruction)).item() > 1e-14)
    return 1;
  const auto          loss = n::mse(net.reconstruction, x);
  const auto          g    = n::grad(loss, p.parameters());
  std::vector<double> enc(6), dec(6), ge(2), gd(3);
  double              mse = 0;
  for (int b = 0; b < 2; ++b) {
    double h[2] = {eb[0], eb[1]}, delta[3];
    for (int j = 0; j < 2; ++j) {
      for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
        h[j] += v[3 * b + i] * w[2 * i + j];
      if (h[j] <= 0 || std::abs(h[j] - net.code.at({b, j})) > 1e-14)
        return 1;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
      double z = db[i];
      for (int j = 0; j < 2; ++j)
        z += h[j] * w[2 * i + j];
      double y = 1 / (1 + std::exp(-z)), error = y - v[3 * b + i];
      mse += error * error / 6;
      if (std::abs(y - net.reconstruction.at({b, i})) > 1e-14)
        return 1;
      delta[i] = error * y * (1 - y) / 3;
      gd[i] += delta[i];
      for (int j = 0; j < 2; ++j)
        dec[2 * i + j] += delta[i] * h[j];
    }
    for (int j = 0; j < 2; ++j) {
      double a = 0;
      for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
        a += delta[i] * w[2 * i + j];
      ge[j] += a;
      for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
        enc[2 * i + j] += v[3 * b + i] * a;
    }
  }
  if (p.count() != 11 || std::abs(loss.item() - mse) > 1e-14)
    return 1;
  for (int j = 0; j < 2; ++j)
    if (std::abs(g[1].at({j}) - ge[j]) > 1e-14)
      return 1;
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
    if (std::abs(g[2].at({i}) - gd[i]) > 1e-14)
      return 1;
  std::vector<double> total, indices{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
  std::print(
      "Hand MSE {:.9f}; code {}.\n\n| W coordinate | encoder path | decoder path | sum "
      "|\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n",
      loss.item(),
      net.code.values_str());
  for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) {
    total.push_back(enc[i] + dec[i]);
    if (std::abs(g[0].at({i / 2, i % 2}) - total[i]) > 1e-14)
      return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} |\n", i, enc[i], dec[i], total[i]);
  }
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.lines(indices, enc, "encoder use", {37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .lines(indices, dec, "decoder use", {220, 38, 38}, 2)
      .lines(indices, total, "shared sum", {22, 163, 74}, 2)
      .x_label("row-major W coordinate")
      .y_label("dL / dW")
      .title("Two nonzero contributions to each shared coordinate")
      .save_svg("build/tied_toy.svg");
}
```
