> Verification and limits

Native tests check all original toy activities and eleven analytic gradients, including both
nonzero shared contributions. Every parameter of tied and untied small models is checked by
finite differences; input and standalone-code first/second derivatives pass away from ReLU
kinks. Every update preserves the exact transpose while independent updates can diverge.
Decoder-only tests exclude encoder bias and preserve signed-code gradients; language tests
retain output fields after parent collection and verify aliases after optimizer updates.
All initial, first-gradient/update, completed-epoch, selected and frozen-output arrays are
compared with the saved original and normal Python/R runs. The optional development NumPy
oracle checks full first-batch derivatives, first Adam updates, every validation checkpoint and
pool/control predictions without becoming a report runtime dependency.

This is one fixed seed and three epochs on a balanced subset. Tying reduces independent
parameters while leaving two matrix multiplications and different optimization dynamics.
No orthogonality, PCA equivalence, inverse decoder, global optimum or universal generalization
benefit follows from the constraint. The measured weak outputs and single-run difference
remain part of the result.
